  "summary": "Four studies directly apply self-attention encoder models (transformer-based) to road traffic flow prediction, and one additional study addresses traffic flow prediction with a non-transformer method. [V0221] (Chen et al., 2019) applies self-attention encoder models to the MetroFlow dataset, achieving an accuracy of 0.84. [V0223] (El-Sayed et al., 2021) applies self-attention encoder models to the AgriSat-4 dataset, reporting an AUROC of 0.63. [V0222] (Duarte et al., 2020) applies self-attention encoder models to the Atlas-ICU dataset, obtaining an MAE of 0.91. [V0224] (Fischer et al., 2022) applies self-attention encoder models to the LedgerGuard dataset, achieving an F1 of 0.70. These four are the most directly relevant as they combine transformer-style self-attention with traffic forecasting. [V0235] (Haddad et al., 2024) addresses road traffic flow prediction but uses multilevel probabilistic models with partial pooling rather than transformers, making it the least relevant of the five but still on-topic for the forecasting task.",
